Z||000000||Greg Piet||11-02-1999||08:29 AM||||I have an old Blaupunkt Santa Fe with a 10 dics changer( I have grown rather attached to the system).   The changer is showing signs of wear(especially in colder weather).  I ahve already tried to disassemble it and grease it up, but to no avail.  Anyone know where I can get a replacement changer for it?<BR>(I know this isnt a fiero question, but it is for a stereo in my fiero)||||reg||1
Z||000001||Ditz88GT||11-02-1999||11:06 AM||||Greg,<P>I had a Blaupunkt Woodstock and a 12 disc changer which I bought in 1990.  About three months ago it finally died.  I contacted Blau to see if I could get a replacement head unit that would work with my changer.  Keep in mind I had this stereo for the better part of 10 years.  I was not ready to give it up.  Well the fine folks at blau told me that my changer was 3 generations out of date and 8 years discontinued.  I guess it was time to give up.  I wish I could tell you to contact Blaupunkt but I fear you would get the same bad news as I got.  But on a brighter note I have seen many blaus going off pretty cheap on ebay.  Good luck, I hope you make out better than I did.<P>Ditz88gt||||reg||3
Z||000002||Cliff Pennock||11-02-1999||01:05 PM||||I just wish I had bought the Delco CD-System back in '89 when I spotted it in the States. It looked exactly the same as the factory radio but with a CD player instead of a cassette. I can remember it being pretty expensive though... Around $1000.||||reg||9
Z||000003||88 Fiero||11-02-1999||03:33 PM||||Cliff you can use the Pontiac Sunfire on a Fiero or the Trans Am... A buddy of mine got one on his Fiero and it looks great....CD and Cassette..||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||11-02-1999||05:20 PM||||nearly all the GM brick stereos are interchangable. There are 2 styles of connector tho. If you want to put a new style connector radio in an old car without cutting you need the connector off the donnor car, and one of the after market radio install plugs. connect the 2 plugs and your in.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Racer 1||11-04-1999||02:39 AM||||I like my Kenwood cd changer its old and still works good. Got it at a pawn shop with a 501 cassette head unit for $200.00..<BR> <A HREF="http://fierorob.homepage.com" TARGET=_blank>http://fierorob.homepage.com</A> <P>||||reg||14
Z||000006||WisconsinGT||11-04-1999||10:34 AM||||I too am in the market for a stereo system for my car. I am thinking newer Delco stereo. I called my local Pontiac dealer. He gave me the number to a company that supplies the dealers with Delco units. They quoted me a price $410.00 for a new CD/Equalizer unit. When I told them it was going into a fiero, they said that they would not offer a warranty. FWIW||||reg||1
Z||000007||deceler8||11-04-1999||12:14 PM||||Has anyone had any experience with the RF modulated CD changers hooked to the stock Delco AM/FM cassette radio ?<P>I want to keep mine stock, mainly to reduce chance of theft, but wonder if they work okay.||||reg||5
Z||000008||Cliff Pennock||11-04-1999||12:24 PM||||WisconsinGT, did they say they offer no warranty <B>because</B> it would be installed in a Fiero? If so, they should be shot! Or at least reported to the BBB.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Raydar||11-04-1999||08:31 PM||||Check out this link.  <A HREF="http://www.aatechnologies.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.aatechnologies.com/</A> <BR>Present and past years radios. They don't list anything specifically for a Fiero, but most any GM radio will fit. They can probably make a recommendation. <BR>I never have purchased anything from them, but their prices sure seem reasonable.||||reg||3
Z||000010||RossT||11-05-1999||12:39 AM||||Great link Raydar. Some questions come up<BR>as to puting in one of the cd/eq radios into a 88 fiero.<P>1. do the radios from the mid 90's pontiac's<BR>   also have orange lights with the blue   <BR>   frequency indicator?<P>2. do the radios from the mid 90's pontiac's<BR>   have more watts per chanal than came stock<BR>   with the fiero?<P>Thanks for any input.  Ross||||reg||1
Z||000011||DJRice||11-09-1999||05:42 PM||||I had a sony RF Mod Changer in my 84 Buick and it worked well, but I think that the radio was so old that it couldnt support ultra high freqs on the antenna input.  Everytime I played a CD that had a cymbal crash or something else high, I would hear a crazy scratching noise.  <P>Other than that, It worked well.||||reg||1
